{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Data Backup"}, {"type": "position", "content": 10}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear or use to talk about the importance of back up."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Implement data migration processes and data backups within the IT solution.  Understand the different types of data movements, making an emphasis on data migration movements as well as data backups. "}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on software testing, test estimation, and data migration processes"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n6le03ob10re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for ideas about the importance of \u2018back up' when saving files."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create a mind map in which you explain what a \u2018back up is' and its importance. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Include other elements that can be backed up (use real life examples)"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share your mind maps with your classmates. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I search about the importance of a \u2018back up'. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I make a mind map to explain a 'back up' in a simple way."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I include real life examples of elements to back up."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I show my mind map to classmates to discuss ideas."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Present Perfect Continuous vs Present Perfect"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p><strong>Present perfect continuous </strong>and<strong> present perfect</strong> can be used to talk about actions that started in the past but affect the present.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>Both tenses are used to talk about actions that started in the past but continue in the present or affect it somehow.</p> <p>In<strong> present perfect continuous</strong>, the action started in the past and continues to the present. In <strong>present perfect</strong>, the action started in the past; and affects the present in some way. It is not certain if the action will continue in the future.</p> <p>The <strong>present perfect</strong> uses the following structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Subject + have/has + past participle</strong></p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>I <strong>have walked</strong> in that park several times.</i></p> <p>And the <strong>present perfect continuous</strong> uses this structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Subject + have/has + been + verb with &ndash;ing</strong></p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>I <strong>have been walking</strong> in that park to reduce my weight.</i></p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Right, but there are cloud providers that have been offering services where VMs can be transferred."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I've been continuously updating my info for two years."}, {"type": "example", "content": "People have experienced the loss of such documents and they can't be easily restored."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Modal Verbs for Speculating - Should"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>There are <strong>modal verbs </strong>used to make assumptions about something.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>In the English language, there are verbs with different functions depending on the context and tense they are used. In some cases, modal verbs express speculation, it means, something that can possibly happen.</p> <p>Some speculation modal verbs are <strong>can</strong>, <strong>could</strong>, <strong>may</strong> and <strong>might</strong>. Furthermore, <strong>should</strong> is a modal verb that can also be used this way, though it&rsquo;s less common.</p> <p>In this case, it&rsquo;s very important to pay attention to the context to find out if its obligation, a suggestion, or an assumption.</p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Sure! The test should be a little bit difficult. We'd better review the topics."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Though technology should be evolving continuously, it's better to prepare for disaster."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "Virtual Machine "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a simulator of an operative system, program, etc."}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Well, public cloud providers allow users to move data, even entire Virtual Machines."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n6le03ob10aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "block "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "structured data with a maximum length which is used as a measure as well "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Don't forget that blocks of data can also be transferred or migrated."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n6le03ob10aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "up-to-date "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "something that exists according to the current time and trends "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Having up-to-date data is always important and very practical."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n6le03ob10aud03"}], "type"